] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fae4dc0d-5fcc-556e-856f-aa7c560289fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-214907" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb311261eabb4b5c1b6c4114c92e798f0caf2e326dc90486429d230167f6978b.png)\n\nExpress the shaded region above as an inequality.The shaded region above is described by the inequality\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-2,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx>-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220c4644-028c-5dcb-955d-3b2d5604dc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395785} {:answer/id #uuid "13e59b84-50f4-570b-b93f-907264edf062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395786} {:answer/id #uuid "23b5a4de-82e3-5600-a5e2-8553e265a482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395787} {:answer/id #uuid "075ca13a-66f5-514e-be5e-b8fce546f7e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395788} {:answer/id #uuid "0135acbf-05a9-5225-8572-13c90063bbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e59b84-50f4-570b-b93f-907264edf062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395786} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6ea143-756a-5eb6-a6ed-9e3449dbdb6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bb098c4-0b9d-56fe-8eb4-70ba83a986f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-21496" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{48}{x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{48}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{48}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{4}-48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{4}-16) & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2$ is a stationary point on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}-\\frac{48}{{1}^{2}}=-45<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-\\frac{48}{{3}^{2}}=27-\\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{65}{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184139 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecbf45e4-67a6-58cd-9655-4a339de64db5" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities Involving Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "658db8cb-ba47-576d-98bf-392b1c972351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390420} {:answer/id #uuid "9a199d3b-122e-5520-ab63-cad8960ce2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390421} {:answer/id #uuid "d68d8126-7623-5eaf-bd8f-5ac5d9474dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390422} {:answer/id #uuid "823ad81d-b696-522f-ab58-29ea625c84a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d68d8126-7623-5eaf-bd8f-5ac5d9474dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390422} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a5871fe-f6ee-5364-9ea7-6831196e61f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "603761b4-58e0-582d-bb8b-85f71e2feec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390425} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4374b4-87ee-5f43-8d23-b5167780b2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4374b4-87ee-5f43-8d23-b5167780b2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390426} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba292289-f6b9-5d5c-b56b-a6376634f5af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84d30d8f-6033-5842-95cc-601ebb4c6d51" :question/math-academy-id "q-215437" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb24be727a3bb001e25b5f2c758b1302332aa8b45a51cfd0d4884b2784479e58.png)\n\nExpress the shaded region above as an inequality.\nThe shaded region above is described by the inequality\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal\" inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *left* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *less than or equal to* those on the line $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx\\le -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2f9488-176e-5ee3-ad8e-2db95b1123f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288350} {:answer/id #uuid "3f03d5e0-e5b1-53b3-ab18-0d7e379a24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288461} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2b0ef-9634-5c51-86e7-60eaa13bc504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289396} {:answer/id #uuid "cea07821-893d-5fac-a3e9-4d99a591dc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289410} {:answer/id #uuid "f789d1aa-f223-5771-a67e-fc052240756a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f03d5e0-e5b1-53b3-ab18-0d7e379a24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288461} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed86ac2-1482-5201-9d78-def088012c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da3fa1e4-9073-5723-b4d9-3e3d9f56d008" :question/math-academy-id "q-215890" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $1.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-x+9$ has a slope of $-1.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-x+9$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28be3240-f527-5881-b030-d0e2f6f588f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=9,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288304} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd4f05d-ba10-5b34-a545-fb122617cedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\,q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288324} {:answer/id #uuid "3c45f0cb-960d-58d2-8635-289e83d41c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288448} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeddfa0-2016-5856-83e5-03c446213f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288578} {:answer/id #uuid "c538b0db-744e-5c84-a6a0-48a8617f619c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c538b0db-744e-5c84-a6a0-48a8617f619c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289338} :answer-field/id #uuid "3459c60d-978a-5a3f-861a-110bccb1e94a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "906fc64a-e7c8-5770-89dc-d9c9f6b082d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-216042" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-15p+4q=-5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $4q$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-4q$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $q$-variable, and allows us to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15p+\\cancel{4q} & =-5 \\\\ 9p-\\cancel{4q} & =-13 \\\\ -6p & =-18 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $p=3$ into the equation $9p-4q=-13$ to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9p-4q & =-13 \\\\ 9\\cdot 3-4q & =-13 \\\\ 27-4q & =-13 \\\\ -4q & =-40 \\\\ q & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346358f5-312e-5c8a-9238-6be633b31e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sin⁡x-cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288379} {:answer/id #uuid "419bbacf-36c2-544f-888a-5bf28372bd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288478} {:answer/id #uuid "70205983-7a23-51c1-a345-bf2ec479a0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288812} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4296e8-700e-5668-8c90-e562531a6962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289261} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a193d2-0ce7-5a28-86c4-834fae7f7271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x-cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba4296e8-700e-5668-8c90-e562531a6962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289261} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c59fbc8-d124-507b-af30-f117e1976ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d70818e9-1705-53df-abe3-9e6e72f287c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2161" :question/problem "$\\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-cos⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sin⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =sin⁡x-(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88c81beb-9b15-56ae-bb46-ce5cbc1d0ea5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using Anticommutativity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c94236-9a9d-5f33-bc42-c13f3f3d00da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j+6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288241} {:answer/id #uuid "782c00ee-c3ed-5215-8945-490b1d847461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i-6j+3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288860} {:answer/id #uuid "8511b2c3-3a1e-5a91-a8c1-2e4b0c95b42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i+6j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288925} {:answer/id #uuid "d8462a2f-c8d3-51af-9bb3-9e0b9ea4371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i-9j+6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289473} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b87c52-4e18-5d4e-9886-3a0c1708e990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+3j-3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "782c00ee-c3ed-5215-8945-490b1d847461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i-6j+3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288860} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bfefd3-5294-5674-b5e8-734bef14e9da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0f3ebcb-819b-5060-93c1-2a4bba4f43b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-216349" :question/problem "Find $a\\times (2a+3b)$, if $b\\times a=-i+2j-k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\na\\times (2a+3b)=a\\times (2a)+a\\times (3b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\na\\times (2a)+a\\times (3b)=2(a\\times a)+3(a\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $a\\times a=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\times a)+3(a\\times b) & =2(0)+3(-b\\times a) \\\\  & =-3(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $b\\times a$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(b\\times a) & =-3(-i+2j-k) \\\\  & =3i-6j+3k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eab183-e6c8-5d48-8660-63dbeb2fb79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{17\\pi \\text{i}/10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288057} {:answer/id #uuid "513eac0f-c2eb-5632-9673-6390df9e0b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288595} {:answer/id #uuid "68f5478d-8bd0-5de2-90c9-4237c57b14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{9\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288768} {:answer/id #uuid "cab704c9-bfc4-5488-8b8d-98f3fc135888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289376} {:answer/id #uuid "e0640fa5-e0b8-56ba-ae2e-572c0a0a90b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{19\\pi \\text{i}/10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513eac0f-c2eb-5632-9673-6390df9e0b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288595} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15ee4bb-1237-59d9-a976-23603c519d15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d76890b3-3454-5f44-b63a-ea0abe359802" :question/math-academy-id "q-216704" :question/problem "![](images/91/91f4aac82208a2801652c9112f58518e1abc89ed4313601a60f7c183b7757f70.png)\n\nFind the $10$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{10}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/10}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d0/d0fc6866d4bb06ec6cbf20bb719faf722ec3c1efe3fba03e413f51dc1c827e71.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=8.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{8}={e}^{(8)\\pi \\text{i}/5}={e}^{8\\pi \\text{i}/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "791706c1-fc72-5123-9e31-3c7ce30854b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{9\\pi \\text{i}/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288864} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce573f9-48e8-5ac3-aa97-939de73f0108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289090} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fdc674-96ab-5ac8-a0ab-d0809b2008c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289120} {:answer/id #uuid "e0df3657-bbfa-5488-8746-ee68814b11da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289534} {:answer/id #uuid "f9884108-f3e3-5cb0-b016-a9030f916564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2fdc674-96ab-5ac8-a0ab-d0809b2008c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5eae31a-74ea-5e55-88a7-2b9c77bcd639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87c48cd8-6b59-5b2c-8f86-b6fedd6aff90" :question/math-academy-id "q-216706" :question/problem "![](images/89/89d0606f11701cb59685e5ea1540b8ed98ce8cfe371951a675f01a1c6cc5a748.png)\n\nFind the $7$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/34/344d760f2cd08e5e1e1eccc2664e7182d01744ee9b0b15f985c6d7b4e0e5515d.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e3a3ed8-0354-5f4f-89c9-625d38e3ddb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288691} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad99bdc-f0d4-5a04-97c9-680d63f9502a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289070} {:answer/id #uuid "b36ddb2a-bd92-52f9-8cc4-1b618625e41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289216} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d482cf-08e9-5fe6-be00-11761fcd614f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289235} {:answer/id #uuid "d53245fa-a4f6-5abc-b9a4-2d6a0a3a3109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d53245fa-a4f6-5abc-b9a4-2d6a0a3a3109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289449} :answer-field/id #uuid "f65e506f-26ae-56f1-b87a-7435b1f33099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7996f9b1-41da-5b9e-ba97-39356ddebf59" :question/math-academy-id "q-216709" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf78e90c844df2d80ffb3b08476ecd0370ffbbf63050bcabf20265692b2deeb3.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $9$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $9$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/e7/e797056434dc122218bdc501cd9e1d5af93127658daa14f7c38b3bd7be8b877f.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/9}={e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019f383c-4240-5aa1-8167-9eeec6560832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288036} {:answer/id #uuid "3089eabc-41b3-575d-9707-096e1189e460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288352} {:answer/id #uuid "36b865dc-3f00-5c77-8e65-143b51a1cd23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288398} {:answer/id #uuid "3907c5c9-15e2-5165-983c-58c43d4ca77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288423} {:answer/id #uuid "a07a07da-8c7f-5d52-a274-e0fe0101c4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3907c5c9-15e2-5165-983c-58c43d4ca77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288423} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b8d3be4-ff98-5294-8a8c-5e871bca47da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76a645d8-58db-587a-a38f-24be87eafa21" :question/math-academy-id "q-216710" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b575a8b3c4830aa48586b457b45f76ca8df49ecf8036df720f66aaddc857bdaf.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $6$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $6$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/34/34a3bb77a9765cd806a047ad42a7f0719201effdd46659efe7ffd1a94a051910.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{5}={e}^{(5)\\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092361 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43dcbb7e-ba21-506f-a706-bfb28371e0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term Smaller Than a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ecd06ce-ff7a-5c63-80d7-6791d3b60b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288229} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f509c0-ef53-5451-a927-e1535f79d5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $8$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289242} {:answer/id #uuid "bb098e6e-7cc7-5cbe-97e9-d1c2e9d1b7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $9$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289266} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f1dd6c-eee7-5523-ac21-571a49e81cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $10$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289541} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb8be69-dba7-5d19-ad05-28bbf89224ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $11$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ecd06ce-ff7a-5c63-80d7-6791d3b60b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The $12$th term" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288229} :answer-field/id #uuid "735be538-bc1e-5645-9fa7-56422609d0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f64f9f3-48d9-568b-881f-f163dc09ba3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-218498" :question/problem "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,08$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n256,\\,64,\\,16,\\,4,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=256$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{64}{256}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4}\\cdot {4}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={4}^{5-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,08.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ {4}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}≈11.805\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,08.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5927e7-3d1a-5589-9ab4-b01b479f66f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288882} {:answer/id #uuid "a65798db-1640-5ac0-9550-f0316b86d818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289133} {:answer/id #uuid "a71c627c-e9b2-5a11-b21d-b190f3a77006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289138} {:answer/id #uuid "d436ea16-40f5-50d5-8944-8947c0a63c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289436} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4463e3-17c4-52b2-809c-06d7c4b4e286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a65798db-1640-5ac0-9550-f0316b86d818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289133} :answer-field/id #uuid "827ea8a6-3f45-58a9-acce-ef8eb4a48299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4b49482-74ae-5d27-81da-2160ac3cb246" :question/math-academy-id "q-2195" :question/problem "$\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $2x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{13}}{13}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8292c378-f477-55da-80ea-56bd4135cf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288910} {:answer/id #uuid "95fa9c35-2493-581e-bec2-06a09917fa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289035} {:answer/id #uuid "afdc0aa9-1534-5121-892d-7fd982536645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289195} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3234b4-fa2a-5696-8475-0c7cca9ca765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289588} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e33c97-12ab-549d-88a5-9b6b03088a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95fa9c35-2493-581e-bec2-06a09917fa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289035} :answer-field/id #uuid "e798780e-db3f-5004-a9f5-57c606c3d4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f63e1f3-652b-58b6-9207-73ea266277c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-22055" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $X,Y,$ and $Z?$\n\nI. The product $XY$ is well defined\n\nII. The product $YX$ is well defined\n\nIII. The product $YZ$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $X$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $Z$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $X$ and $2$ rows in $Y.$ Since the number of columns in $X$ equals the number of rows in $Y,$ the matrices $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $2$ rows in $X.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $X,$ the matrices $Y$ and $X$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{X}_{2\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $Y$ and $3$ rows in $Z.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ equals the number of rows in $Z,$ the matrices $Y$ and $Z$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Z}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1030c-14e2-59e9-a189-ae72636d7cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288429} {:answer/id #uuid "831fe1aa-d1a9-59de-914e-66c8e45135cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x}ln⁡2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288914} {:answer/id #uuid "a92a1f84-319a-5ffc-912d-da344601d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289153} {:answer/id #uuid "bfec5a66-eb21-510f-ac20-724d2da23f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{x}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289301} {:answer/id #uuid "f611671a-7317-5307-aefb-d9046cadfb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f611671a-7317-5307-aefb-d9046cadfb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b01bf45-11e5-550e-91d5-6d78dcabad9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3b4a92d-a353-507a-becb-bee354a44152" :question/math-academy-id "q-2277" :question/problem "$\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e268375-538a-538a-80bc-2f086cffdbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288224} {:answer/id #uuid "793b6c32-8350-533d-bf9d-724d40064c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{y}}{6x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288865} {:answer/id #uuid "a3589f85-19fc-5bd4-ac3b-96bf622c2422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289122} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d85bbf-5174-563d-a666-9a94bd4cf6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289532} {:answer/id #uuid "e27c90f5-ce7c-52d7-8488-fe25083eb2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e268375-538a-538a-80bc-2f086cffdbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288224} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f58fa9b-a3f6-53aa-a4c8-4680d5988ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "623f14ca-5670-5819-81c3-5ed253df18c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2289" :question/problem "Given that  $y-{2}^{y}=3{x}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y-{2}^{y} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-{2}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2){2}^{y})(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =6x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7b0c8d-b34e-529f-8e57-7e5b410570dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288144} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1e9549-878f-505d-9d1b-2dca4e681cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288674} {:answer/id #uuid "687bd2d9-6711-5dfe-88c4-8d35b364c3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288764} {:answer/id #uuid "c9baa381-d94b-5a2b-bad4-655c2ed22f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289370} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f8ef87-9260-58f5-90d8-84f8075a8fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "687bd2d9-6711-5dfe-88c4-8d35b364c3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288764} :answer-field/id #uuid "8157ac93-f1f0-5a52-861d-c556bc05930c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c15c8a07-d217-56eb-8e6f-77808e46bc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-2305" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+x-2$ on the interval $[-1,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,2)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(-1)}{2-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+2-2)-(3\\cdot (-1)^{2}+(-1)-2)}{2+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+x-2)=6x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=6c+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6c+1 & =4 \\\\ 6c & =3 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129080 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041bb980-2220-5c0f-b904-f70291dc858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288059} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcd216a-5d96-580d-89a1-1300b816ea9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288211} {:answer/id #uuid "6428772c-31cb-5d11-ad40-b384b65de264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288738} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ab4c14-8b8f-5d7f-941d-c5430187106a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289135} {:answer/id #uuid "b4335ae8-54ab-5f8c-98b6-6b1f10f60c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{33}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6428772c-31cb-5d11-ad40-b384b65de264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288738} :answer-field/id #uuid "94cc4b6d-4396-5dd4-a8e0-bcf717fb1b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "808fb241-7b41-5e43-96f3-08a62164f95c" :question/math-academy-id "q-23289" :question/problem "![](images/89/8904a5ebafade54a46cee77a2e20cac255c888d257ef1c208b2def32d247f2cd.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{11}{12}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{11}{12}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{11}{12})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{121}{144}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{121}{144} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{23}{144} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{23}{144}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6238c9-96e3-5e78-8387-788d29941b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288141} {:answer/id #uuid "466bc7c5-9b39-5eb1-bdd3-0b9a00b572ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}\\,,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288514} {:answer/id #uuid "91235981-d37a-5d0d-b5ba-d77f81fb8017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289007} {:answer/id #uuid "94d05c5e-fd78-5cd8-9811-47091fe1b8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\," :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289028} {:answer/id #uuid "b782dc4d-940d-5580-a9f0-f268d13c8bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}\\," :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f6238c9-96e3-5e78-8387-788d29941b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288141} :answer-field/id #uuid "63371e9f-cfd3-5536-b6c2-f282539fa3f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fff73f04-f965-5171-bca1-917e7258f29b" :question/math-academy-id "q-2342" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+5$ on the interval $[1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ and differentiable on $(1,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (1,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{({4}^{2}-2\\cdot 4+5)-({1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+5)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-4}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2x+5)=2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c-2 & =3 \\\\ 2c & =5 \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff8525f-bdb5-59b7-9364-7f3238e455f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288149} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1a00c6-778b-593d-99b5-702f58820750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288203} {:answer/id #uuid "42931aef-7dc3-55d6-82f9-d903adc9adb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288484} {:answer/id #uuid "4c581d2f-fca2-53f4-8e91-541c726d08fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288560} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7bc305-8f2b-5684-a01c-e4bf62ba726c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c581d2f-fca2-53f4-8e91-541c726d08fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288560} :answer-field/id #uuid "b56098b1-fb45-5f85-8ed3-7a8cab96d6c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4551db6-cbed-5a0b-aa5c-4809ad316023" :question/math-academy-id "q-2351" :question/problem "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t$, where $t$ is measured in seconds.  Find the particle's acceleration at $t=2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-6t+2) \\\\  & =12t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=2$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =12(2)-6 \\\\  & =24-6 \\\\  & =18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=2$ seconds is $18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277373e3-169a-586e-ad29-c1c31afaae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288292} {:answer/id #uuid "6a095a67-ddbe-5bf7-9a39-f8782eff2ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288780} {:answer/id #uuid "94d134d0-8d56-5307-b137-977c53f49ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289029} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3d194e-cae3-5d1d-9ecd-a175924b168f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289186} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7343e7-6e46-504c-b190-873fccc910a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7343e7-6e46-504c-b190-873fccc910a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289392} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b2f4eb-6782-5d0a-9ec1-3e356d9f2e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d60ea30f-16e8-5e44-938b-3986249a44fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-23526" :question/problem "$\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "Since both fractions are over a common denominator, we can add them. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ to simplify the expression in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35fe6748-f040-5318-84e0-f3f76c9c7250" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108b997f-3ef8-51bd-8557-0ff04c25f1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288157} {:answer/id #uuid "7041b720-f438-5081-b848-05a44b0aed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288814} {:answer/id #uuid "78afce16-dd3c-5a3e-ac4b-6998edd1f35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288862} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b3fb44-66d6-5d64-93da-9ca291156c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289642} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e503b-835c-559e-adc2-29bda28d6008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7041b720-f438-5081-b848-05a44b0aed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288814} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f020196-633d-58ab-b488-e011bb361efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2a7de17-dd0e-54fa-9258-ee9fa753362a" :question/math-academy-id "q-23602" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ce4bdc-93af-5a54-9a75-4348de1eca82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288056} {:answer/id #uuid "38003279-169b-5abe-bbd8-5dd8613c632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288412} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e5544f-7d20-58e7-8ae2-104f0dc53c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289236} {:answer/id #uuid "c004da3d-3b21-5bf2-8b40-1a7f273f69dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289302} {:answer/id #uuid "c111a5f3-32b9-5d72-a5ee-cf9cbe58a3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5e5544f-7d20-58e7-8ae2-104f0dc53c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289236} :answer-field/id #uuid "a41fbea3-85aa-5b15-9e85-ef6d9ffbc9e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df553b92-e7eb-5bae-8500-5506ac0ebe3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2361" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)=4{t}^{2}-16t+15.$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}-16t+15) \\\\  & =8t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=0,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =8t-16 \\\\ 8t & =16 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=2,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =4(2)^{2}-16(2)+15 \\\\  & =16-32+15 \\\\  & =-1\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $-1\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b644ab2-4c21-5aeb-9032-26a79415192f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288444} {:answer/id #uuid "540e1587-3f3e-52a8-8424-5abf66ff30e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288610} {:answer/id #uuid "70b1df14-3de9-50cb-8b16-2a2eec5b3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816} {:answer/id #uuid "959ae715-31b5-5f44-931c-f40632872d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289030} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d44e35-06ab-5ae0-8bfd-256c7f07808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70b1df14-3de9-50cb-8b16-2a2eec5b3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816} :answer-field/id #uuid "990087b9-37dc-5d9d-b34d-123762544320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4eec6d2-1c29-5a4c-9b34-ea67f65dce06" :question/math-academy-id "q-23621" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+3y=6?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+3y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =6 \\\\ 3y & =-x+6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=3x$ has a slope of $3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=3x$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9cfaf6-9a1a-5e8a-ba94-ac0ace22d609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288209} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ae802-61bf-59f5-8b33-d414ac86732a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288552} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9e515d-8988-5114-8e78-b9e0df04aced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288663} {:answer/id #uuid "6463122b-ca6b-5780-a23a-d3c73e619707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288741} {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec350e8b-707f-561e-b0a8-e7f213d56fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96f3c704-d5cd-55de-a550-62294d23b6d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-23631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line containing the point $(-1,1)$ that is perpendicular to $2x-3y=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $2x-3y=-5$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =-5 \\\\ -3y & =-2x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2y & =-3x-1 \\\\ 3x+2y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "240a0a0b-fb86-5760-bb2b-74275708ff77" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb8834a-50b7-5416-880c-a8fd45d432bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288135} {:answer/id #uuid "13ea8bc8-393e-5910-9dfa-5a1ece3971a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288168} {:answer/id #uuid "526475f0-7a67-5902-ad09-03aaeaa90b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288604} {:answer/id #uuid "69cdaa41-514c-59d8-8b47-dc49563092c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288778} {:answer/id #uuid "d3227cac-a33f-5639-a941-a9ded844e2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ea8bc8-393e-5910-9dfa-5a1ece3971a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288168} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d1ac430-1346-5392-97ab-d61f5c529673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4adf28a2-e005-5bdd-b207-f7c30d8f8ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-23642" :question/problem "Simplify ${csc}^{4}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{4}⁡\\theta -2{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{4}⁡\\theta -2{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={csc}^{4}⁡\\theta -{({cot}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={csc}^{4}⁡\\theta -({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}-2({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={csc}^{4}⁡\\theta -({csc}^{4}⁡\\theta -2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +1)-2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\  & ={csc}^{4}⁡\\theta -{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta +2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1-2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\  & =\\cancel{{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\cancel{{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta }+\\cancel{2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }-1-\\cancel{2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1000e887-f114-51b9-b67a-d436889f9bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288151} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6cbdef-32ec-5499-a6bd-ce420ddec4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288580} {:answer/id #uuid "812d7309-b400-5fd3-bd24-0d8abc7cac68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288903} {:answer/id #uuid "a7847429-4b08-529a-a14c-c8cbd6af9359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289142} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa64626-acf1-57f9-bd83-06342e759333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f6cbdef-32ec-5499-a6bd-ce420ddec4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288580} :answer-field/id #uuid "2edfa40a-17b0-5eff-898c-e2c29a2c872d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71a079d2-97d0-5455-a26c-8d71df225e2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-236734" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨-1,1,2⟩$ and $q=⟨4,x,1⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot x+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129123 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b0d2e17-b1cc-53d6-a590-29ca48abe4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288441} {:answer/id #uuid "4a00572c-39f3-51f2-908d-92627f661500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288544} {:answer/id #uuid "a37b2fb0-553a-530f-99ca-b90f84cdee3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289123} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1273fc-bf67-57d2-a722-130587889698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289164} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f3f20-7128-592d-a073-380a9759c7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0d2e17-b1cc-53d6-a590-29ca48abe4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288441} :answer-field/id #uuid "246102b7-25b3-5a75-9c7c-581da5239b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "250619d1-370b-5c0d-af5b-ed64a801198a" :question/math-academy-id "q-236893" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-7$ and a maximum value of $3.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-7+3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129123 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "933b20c7-323f-5443-9cf4-97e87fa2c6fc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ffe8c2d-22ae-5a74-9fb5-b44fdb8cce26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288150} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9156c5-37a7-56f3-b8d3-991ed27e42a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288465} {:answer/id #uuid "90eda464-9558-5e5e-b442-f50682de504d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289005} {:answer/id #uuid "d650ff67-6889-5b78-bc90-d9ce4271c33c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289456} {:answer/id #uuid "d7360b5c-2ef2-52b6-a3be-6f516c9b4151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90eda464-9558-5e5e-b442-f50682de504d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289005} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0fa4e38-53cb-50b5-ba34-1bae28b400d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "723b13df-bde4-592d-ade7-17cdd1dad568" :question/math-academy-id "q-236896" :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-5$ and a maximum value of $9.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-5+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4385b2-cedb-5f97-84c3-917a017432a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288112} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9f0461-9e12-5816-9c05-6b4fa234ef57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288216} {:answer/id #uuid "58d0b77c-504d-574d-966c-005ac0e0d6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288652} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9bb498-f6e5-5039-b259-d917d5660a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288699} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bd4a4b-0690-5bdb-96fa-3576eebd5a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58d0b77c-504d-574d-966c-005ac0e0d6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288652} :answer-field/id #uuid "057f72f3-32e2-5bf8-9e0a-6bb801c5adf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7caa2144-9fd2-5d73-bd9e-432e5c2fb4d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-236971" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 3x & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc40103-4688-55dd-8d82-093d855eb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288339} {:answer/id #uuid "4be5c6a7-9473-5f8f-a76d-361e76618b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288557} {:answer/id #uuid "969e8fda-7e9c-55a7-bced-44ceb4371dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289039} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe15376-5215-5280-ae06-1a3991f84d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289384} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4d7bb2-b033-5091-b1bf-ce3cd9d629f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dc40103-4688-55dd-8d82-093d855eb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288339} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bd2ffd0-af54-5576-8d75-4029636169bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23ed6766-8e64-5c6e-b8a5-b8f25dcf697a" :question/math-academy-id "q-237018" :question/problem "Let $a=5i-4j+3k$ and $b=-7i+xj+k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-7)+(-4)\\cdot x+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -4x-32 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-32 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341784da-b585-53fe-93fa-c3cbb0d2b032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288377} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca536b9-617f-5625-ad14-acd349960cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288973} {:answer/id #uuid "ac275e68-b65e-502d-8d04-712cba1e1a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289174} {:answer/id #uuid "bba33e30-0659-53ad-a553-8814871bb2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289270} {:answer/id #uuid "f03674f0-c895-5b67-901a-d1feb586a561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "341784da-b585-53fe-93fa-c3cbb0d2b032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288377} :answer-field/id #uuid "fff57b46-70ef-54bd-87fb-a12c9a20446e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8158669b-62f4-54f1-8025-20269eb7a332" :question/math-academy-id "q-237020" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $q=⟨3,0,x⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 3x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c8fa6e-ec22-57a4-ace9-9ab79eb86dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288485} {:answer/id #uuid "b84d2b62-4353-5f5a-a5c9-69ea117204f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289251} {:answer/id #uuid "d5049764-b1b5-5357-be51-767024d656d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289448} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf67f99-cbea-58b8-a183-8ed451369ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289496} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4a724f-68fe-5924-b70a-bdc7366c3c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5049764-b1b5-5357-be51-767024d656d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289448} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc69ae9-136f-5a66-9a05-68c04a2bca60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "487960be-d7be-5dc4-ae5e-e966e5d0db00" :question/math-academy-id "q-237022" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,5,x⟩$ and $q=⟨-3,1,2⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045ea7df-d48b-5101-9c0c-7774dc6805c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288062} {:answer/id #uuid "4d31c233-2e4c-57f7-9920-b65073d0d7ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288566} {:answer/id #uuid "64c5cf3d-8049-5124-af6a-dce315f146c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288745} {:answer/id #uuid "7951f08c-4d89-5398-9695-a3e5c15037b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288867} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ccec9a-d724-59c9-bf5f-e71a3038702e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c5cf3d-8049-5124-af6a-dce315f146c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288745} :answer-field/id #uuid "008e8235-fe77-52a6-94f1-aa6a712b1be3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a183fbf8-0afb-55d8-9c23-9f82dba8cb3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-23799" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta $ using its definition and simplify further:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91100afb-9a36-57c0-9f30-4692cfef0b93" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent and Other Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9f8a16-ff09-5f79-a6bf-cbb7ae4b2cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288574} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd70fe9-cd2b-54a0-92ed-673217cf8585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288808} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc9663d-cbb3-5fdb-a3c0-1f14b51a5538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289088} {:answer/id #uuid "bf53601a-1c08-59ec-9a42-2570f16e5525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289293} {:answer/id #uuid "efc6802f-4229-5f81-ab18-3d63117f754c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fd70fe9-cd2b-54a0-92ed-673217cf8585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288808} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ec8b3f-8125-5231-aebe-9acb5e4b9349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2b71b00-c11b-5c0b-9ab2-a575a74118da" :question/math-academy-id "q-23847" :question/problem "$({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nAlso, recall the Pythagorean formula for sines and cosines:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x & =1+{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "240a0a0b-fb86-5760-bb2b-74275708ff77" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074fdb0b-a308-592d-95a7-627490475229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288090} {:answer/id #uuid "5783022f-4924-5f6c-9cae-555095fa0e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288640} {:answer/id #uuid "90b88db6-bab3-5a4c-99fa-584338a46a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289002} {:answer/id #uuid "cf687924-3c7d-5b1e-a02e-efa81c08aab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289416} {:answer/id #uuid "db309d7c-29f6-5802-ae1d-a51c0aae9f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5783022f-4924-5f6c-9cae-555095fa0e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288640} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1dd2961-41df-5267-b65a-47c8ee4b24c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a349287-5d4c-537c-9bb0-00fd8c054f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-23849" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we operate the first two terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{({cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{-({csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-({csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{-1}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "141d61c7-1d82-52fe-b977-19daa910da9b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring Alternative Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47cb1ec6-7258-5391-bba7-eddd1709db30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288525} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8d72a9-6e73-515a-854b-4f57d5fcacdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288549} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebdf1f9-0a5f-5a05-a4cc-707aff2447eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288987} {:answer/id #uuid "a105dd5b-7398-57b0-9f26-b6bec2f24a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289108} {:answer/id #uuid "fa411dac-4ab5-5b47-81f9-8021d5039ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a105dd5b-7398-57b0-9f26-b6bec2f24a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289108} :answer-field/id #uuid "1532933f-2925-54d8-9ae2-1303326ed3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a01be135-3059-50f3-96f4-e6908bb8d8f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-23853" :question/problem "Simplify ${sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{4}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify our expression by factoring it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{4}⁡x & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+({tan}^{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{4}⁡x=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03e934c8-da65-5615-9717-a50eee3577fd" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11177df5-0483-5972-bcaf-9017710da256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288159} {:answer/id #uuid "9705b997-814b-59ba-9086-20d3695fb1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289042} {:answer/id #uuid "ddddbc9c-8e13-5c76-8aa8-0be8e56b19f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289507} {:answer/id #uuid "e95a230c-2af6-58ef-bf2b-d28f8c9c3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289581} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2bc56b-6a94-5d2d-a542-0a0c2d7488ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e95a230c-2af6-58ef-bf2b-d28f8c9c3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289581} :answer-field/id #uuid "27abd601-d10e-5e6b-9bac-388db9d51c71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87181cd2-9356-511c-8a55-f0b3b8086037" :question/math-academy-id "q-2389" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{3}+7.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d012a04e-f802-5a26-b900-0801427073d8" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b18c6a05-844b-5206-b3e5-0ce6326b0d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289206} {:answer/id #uuid "b436c6f7-d646-5b9f-aa11-5941dcdc990c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{3}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289225} {:answer/id #uuid "d8589411-fbdf-5b50-9eba-de99e34580ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289474} {:answer/id #uuid "d86ac2a5-c6c6-5345-9657-3860d675b972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289476} {:answer/id #uuid "fec95ded-39dc-5c20-9496-9dcb8bfbcf09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18c6a05-844b-5206-b3e5-0ce6326b0d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b30197c-f6f6-5ce8-b1f2-2d38394819a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2d6dd75-f063-5feb-ad58-0c61bba9a5c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-23897" :question/problem "Simplify $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =[1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ]+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor out ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ and we use the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ again. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\, \\\\  & ={csc}^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d012a04e-f802-5a26-b900-0801427073d8" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c11a98-a429-5f8c-9801-3e71352bfb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288285} {:answer/id #uuid "3897ff35-e87a-5a29-9032-5e07bd0c4985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288419} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3ffb47-3276-5091-a415-fa8fec9ce38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288683} {:answer/id #uuid "d455067b-0dec-5c44-8118-9b5b1ff428e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289440} {:answer/id #uuid "f9aa2d21-a95b-5bbf-8025-7e8589c6fa4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3897ff35-e87a-5a29-9032-5e07bd0c4985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288419} :answer-field/id #uuid "17bab95c-81ea-5471-ab78-f85af95d1be3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ce8a935-1da8-5328-91b9-35c9544cd048" :question/math-academy-id "q-23898" :question/problem "Simplify ${tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the given expression as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor out ${tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and use the identity \n${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={cot}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e395c8-f565-5740-959d-6bf0c34f8710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288420} {:answer/id #uuid "47d8a58a-8445-5585-bc47-18b1d4683989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288526} {:answer/id #uuid "60da0eec-e09f-556c-977c-0afa9bca8118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288710} {:answer/id #uuid "9efacfe8-4ef9-586f-935e-d85e18391332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289098} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f42cff-705d-5052-8de2-d67ef0b14a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sec}^{2}⁡x+3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38e395c8-f565-5740-959d-6bf0c34f8710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288420} :answer-field/id #uuid "84733a66-1c91-5acb-9908-573c1e35d199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4cb91b7-6460-5845-ba44-f35610f01889" :question/math-academy-id "q-2392" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={a}^{3x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $v(x)={a}^{3x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)({a}^{3x})+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a) \\\\  & ={a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018421ea-dca9-5daf-ab3a-c27696ce4249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288034} {:answer/id #uuid "1e02db35-b595-59ea-973e-bd157371128c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288223} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5c0bc2-0072-5944-8dd3-fe8dcbae8938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288693} {:answer/id #uuid "ac81cfb0-7259-5f57-9adb-04296e897ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289177} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7a7d1a-20e3-5bd6-9408-f184d8d4065a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac81cfb0-7259-5f57-9adb-04296e897ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f45efbdd-39dd-5ca7-b621-baab9c7920f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3d7d091-4a85-557a-b384-1cb4715f620a" :question/math-academy-id "q-2403" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}})(2x)-(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})(2)}{(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcd191d-3785-54a0-a639-e0094f61d3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288129} {:answer/id #uuid "6905831a-aab1-5aa6-90c9-108a514050cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288770} {:answer/id #uuid "78f720c7-027b-52c2-b6ef-5787c36db04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288863} {:answer/id #uuid "978813b7-39da-53a5-9479-68b97e87dbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289046} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb5517e-f727-547d-b85c-617c8b602edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978813b7-39da-53a5-9479-68b97e87dbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289046} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c40079c-8840-5be3-8c8b-803492714f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "399f64e6-0f82-5ca3-861d-fbe99de11ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-242923" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${3}^{2x}={8}^{1-6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2x} & ={8}^{1-6x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{1-6x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6c484f-0d4c-5059-abdc-0a5388322664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288449} {:answer/id #uuid "905767c2-2f4f-53cd-a805-da5f17ade7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288998} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d3b909-fd08-568d-a4fd-3a367cf58a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289118} {:answer/id #uuid "b27cd6d7-da62-5d7b-84c9-e5730cb22b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289211} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c5c2bc-0b3f-5d28-8a4b-51fdecf0f8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2d3b909-fd08-568d-a4fd-3a367cf58a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289118} :answer-field/id #uuid "f665bcbb-88dc-5ad6-8703-67a0c36bad75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a509f6a5-78b4-503c-a592-ded49f6e4644" :question/math-academy-id "q-24341" :question/problem "Express $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dff5b7c-544a-5fda-a175-51ce4723a135" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1383fd97-77d3-5ac2-be23-4b9d7dc8885a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288167} {:answer/id #uuid "362fcbae-cb89-5509-8424-b1ee7253363f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\le x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288395} {:answer/id #uuid "46d5b3e6-49b1-5f02-8bd8-ebc685df8719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288519} {:answer/id #uuid "4881613a-3587-5b83-92d1-c4557759480e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288534} {:answer/id #uuid "570c0835-b65c-52fe-9f64-104b4ace6481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46d5b3e6-49b1-5f02-8bd8-ebc685df8719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288519} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a561c7b-ae6b-533f-be16-6d5b7c106d68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffe39127-4650-5f93-972b-bb5efc38f550" :question/math-academy-id "q-24351" :question/problem "![](images/09/098189dc8e96b01cb045a198eef3f00ba2df6594fc14675cad81246fc2d25964.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x+2)(x-3)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-3)\\ge 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-3)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e80a6e08ccff87314e5081a08332f0de508f6db02446742d1c93416d41a4b3c.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fe34d4f-b1fc-5ae0-9d55-04367e3e3a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ pounds per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288703} {:answer/id #uuid "8837d663-da3e-54d6-ad9e-b7fbd44fd2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ pounds per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288950} {:answer/id #uuid "b1646ebc-8eb5-5ea1-9807-4559284424b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ pounds per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289205} {:answer/id #uuid "bf59944a-828b-52d4-8152-acc3370d61a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ pounds per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289294} {:answer/id #uuid "da9443d8-9b09-5781-bcc9-9bb6ff7ade2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15$ pounds per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1646ebc-8eb5-5ea1-9807-4559284424b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ pounds per year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289205} :answer-field/id #uuid "4197b5aa-e931-53da-b252-0e4b8f548ea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89c5ed41-56cc-572f-ab21-6b6ef12532fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-2439" :question/problem "The weight $w,$ in pounds, of a baby gorilla $t$ years after birth can be modeled by the function $w(t)=4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+15.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of his weight when $t=1$ year?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of weight when $t=1$ year is equal to ${w}^{′}(1).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+15) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(1) & =12(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =12-2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the weight when $t=1$ year is $10$ pounds per year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7088b1af-54df-5c95-bec1-e96b53c5f1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288815} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca70449-d3c0-500a-bf01-9fc59bdb8e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289086} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1691b5-d526-5306-8cf2-8c5f601c7ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289165} {:answer/id #uuid "e0db6555-c3dc-5b31-9920-d1231647197e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289533} {:answer/id #uuid "e474e08f-fd81-5207-ab8d-f763fcb42bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e474e08f-fd81-5207-ab8d-f763fcb42bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289548} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da4ca44-fffb-53ef-ac9c-e81ad6738ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a18ec028-af86-5940-bde5-98a13535f55f" :question/math-academy-id "q-24490" :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ec37c5e-8a40-58a7-8af3-4457cd5da43f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43eca4f5-7e71-5c4d-b3d7-7641c44eb318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288495} {:answer/id #uuid "492ca887-fe22-579a-b5cb-0f29cfad327c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288539} {:answer/id #uuid "93678e0d-634c-5d71-b943-62299e286df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289019} {:answer/id #uuid "c7df7cd8-b50b-55b6-b605-e2f558905842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289355} {:answer/id #uuid "c878a2ea-30a4-57ca-8bad-92bcefc1de12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "492ca887-fe22-579a-b5cb-0f29cfad327c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288539} :answer-field/id #uuid "44b91e88-c247-552d-ac22-5e6fb9335e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccbcdf42-d0d6-5ef1-b1a3-dd2467d078f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-24499" :question/problem "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2902b205-4508-55c6-9a68-f57d3afc9eab" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values of Trigonometric Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0043b9dd-acd7-5722-ae3f-3f717ea8f717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288026} {:answer/id #uuid "0fcd1b2f-6684-5235-9ad9-1439089c5d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288147} {:answer/id #uuid "2e93b461-8b28-554d-a5d3-b0197fe38c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288346} {:answer/id #uuid "38664848-58db-58e9-955b-e673ca767957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288415} {:answer/id #uuid "679b2481-2702-5446-9e64-41947ec19124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679b2481-2702-5446-9e64-41947ec19124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288759} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e2b6c24-7b3b-5d04-808b-ab04891dc6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baeab088-833a-54f7-96df-e4a2e1a80c83" :question/math-academy-id "q-24514" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} {:answer/id #uuid "99103521-803c-5804-a165-119989cc93da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289057} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f66a3c-9003-56a0-98dc-04353efeac62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} {:answer/id #uuid "c824007d-3cd4-55bb-995f-0ebfb53b60b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289358} {:answer/id #uuid "e69d7548-01ac-54b0-b01f-7d230602a3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} :answer-field/id #uuid "0da5f05f-ddec-577d-bb96-889893b10755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50632a56-05a3-5b2c-a470-4387daec2008" :question/math-academy-id "q-24574" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c635f8-a836-5e71-b883-8a1bd5cf25e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288196} {:answer/id #uuid "2bacbcf5-685c-51b1-88e0-85a788eecc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288318} {:answer/id #uuid "87cc2f4e-738c-52b7-abe2-f860d6aed289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288948} {:answer/id #uuid "9086facf-67d8-55b3-ba11-e7e20d058362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288999} {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432} :answer-field/id #uuid "468c38a2-26d6-518c-b2b0-6ef0ade60949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca1f80dd-221f-5c68-b74a-e39a400a3763" :question/math-academy-id "q-24575" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3315c5-e581-5704-8feb-0fa7e40d938b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288885} {:answer/id #uuid "af7ea6ab-b4ce-5db0-93b2-7941e17fe43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289193} {:answer/id #uuid "e910a38f-f4e4-5977-9d9f-7ed4f36b6aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289579} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b76b41-917c-54ab-ab82-87ce9743027a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d607bd-1288-57e3-afbf-e5c537389a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd03cb1f-af7d-59db-bf84-fa1a13cb0a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-24576" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062a3218-733e-525a-8b5d-331d7e8996a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288081} {:answer/id #uuid "24557d4c-6a4a-5cd5-a7e3-e24950b0390f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288265} {:answer/id #uuid "2d84974c-0049-5521-a257-45ab79f50d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288337} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ea36f8-fdb9-5210-9922-102972d65fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289467} {:answer/id #uuid "f0888764-f8f2-5ce9-b665-20d788007d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0888764-f8f2-5ce9-b665-20d788007d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289621} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91ed20d-b388-5b5f-8238-6bf59161d259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4eaa49cb-94d5-5c4a-8e77-4592aca20df1" :question/math-academy-id "q-24641" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={30}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {30}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-1+{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity in the form ${sin}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}=1-{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})\\cancel{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "250cdf52-80dd-5fc2-af4b-70ef347a1cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288273} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd9b186-aadf-56c5-aa56-1a4655188af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288467} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4e972a-a6ae-5ee9-939d-472e588fddcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289160} {:answer/id #uuid "dd44e3ed-170a-5c64-abc8-da906810e3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289505} {:answer/id #uuid "e97836dd-63c8-53a9-9a9c-9ddec55ceab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd9b186-aadf-56c5-aa56-1a4655188af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288467} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8d3d20-451b-5d5a-b8e5-ba16d376b2a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4270d786-b50e-5319-88f8-bca386712273" :question/math-academy-id "q-24645" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "143766e0-0ef5-5caf-b699-d394d4a707e9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c357368-fcee-57c4-bf91-a6420fe440cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288213} {:answer/id #uuid "27f10174-6b5a-51a5-a3ff-b27f66395464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288298} {:answer/id #uuid "853e171a-cb9a-5680-8257-0e827c783b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288930} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2b8d46-c755-5546-ae37-7ed12f4842f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289390} {:answer/id #uuid "e5710b37-6161-53d9-9349-b0d8e0d217e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc2b8d46-c755-5546-ae37-7ed12f4842f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289390} :answer-field/id #uuid "f984848b-f15f-5efc-b09a-0122e1e107ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feecaade-207a-51bf-96ee-61bce1de2e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-24647" :question/problem "Express $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21623035-5d3b-54b0-b966-85086e882ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288244} {:answer/id #uuid "327ce358-2120-579c-a825-a49f79276978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288362} {:answer/id #uuid "462fdeb4-069c-543e-84f4-6256b0c7292e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288512} {:answer/id #uuid "4691cd9d-d70d-5609-bda6-07f12d1b3808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288515} {:answer/id #uuid "6381d420-eb79-5f12-aa11-1fa09a350b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4691cd9d-d70d-5609-bda6-07f12d1b3808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288515} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e9dae2-f4ee-56c2-b632-6441b7a7809a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f30309d1-f3e3-55ac-be8a-acbe0c0f980a" :question/math-academy-id "q-24650" :question/problem "$8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}) & =4\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9ad609-8d7b-5e63-94bf-7cfba3aec5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288460} {:answer/id #uuid "abf31e5b-9e7a-5b32-8ddf-d15f59773d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289170} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aa96ca-5fb9-50d0-8d56-f3836db4b900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289207} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a024b9-8648-5ee6-8075-33884e637425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289213} {:answer/id #uuid "e6200ebf-b364-55da-8734-0d50031c5a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a024b9-8648-5ee6-8075-33884e637425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289213} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ce7f3d-ede3-5d56-84d2-9fd025fc8f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54523433-2b3d-5350-9453-344ec229fc6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2469" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86221215-32a6-5c5a-bcd7-220e166bcee8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c920cf3-116e-56f2-8691-7c743cd4158f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288125} {:answer/id #uuid "315ee77f-8c78-5bab-bec1-2885e27e5936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288355} {:answer/id #uuid "42f68950-1b41-58e2-98a3-e0f70ec16612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288487} {:answer/id #uuid "44bd2ab2-5caf-5814-949c-3a7acceb1565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288499} {:answer/id #uuid "e858d4d7-3806-5f1b-9e88-58e3a48b2838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315ee77f-8c78-5bab-bec1-2885e27e5936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288355} :answer-field/id #uuid "9addeaa0-ab7b-5b7e-bed6-8af5d4754d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f0edaee-3cfb-50c7-84ca-c9bc566266d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-2472" :question/problem "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)=2x$ on the interval $[1,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({x}^{2})|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =4 \\\\ 2c & =4 \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
